% technique_id: T1013
% technique_name: Port Monitors
% domain: enterprise
% tactic(s): persistence;privilege-escalation
% platforms: Windows
% url: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1013
% is_subtechnique: no
% stix_id: attack-pattern--1f47e2fd-fa77-4f2f-88ee-e85df308f125
% description: A port monitor can be set through the  (Citation: AddMonitor) API call to set a DLL to be loaded at startup. (Citation: AddMonitor) This DLL can be located in <code>C:\Windows\System32</code> and will be loaded by the print spooler service, spoolsv.exe, on boot. The spoolsv.exe process also runs under SYSTEM level permissions. (Citation: Bloxham) Alternatively, an arbitrary DLL can be loaded if permissions allow writing a fully-qualified pathname for that DLL to <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Print\Monitors</code>.   The Registry key contains entries for the following:  * Local Port * Standard TCP/IP Port * USB Monitor * WSD Port  Adversaries can use this technique to load malicious code at startup that will persist on system reboot and execute as SYSTEM.
% generated_by: notebook_generate_rules

% ---- RULE LLM START (minimal) ----
    persistence(Host, 'port_monitor') :-
        attacker_at(net),
        connects(net, Host, _Port),
        os(Host, 'Windows'),
        setting(Host, 'HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Print\\Monitors', _),
        exec(Host, 'SYSTEM').

    compromise(Host, 'SYSTEM') :-
        persistence(Host, 'port_monitor').
% ---- RULE LLM END ----
